From 5e1c2076120609cd2b113223e2cc6a8fdf039224 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Rajan <7121943+kvnloo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:27:57 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): keep a stale no-owner tile recovery from
 latching a live session read-only

A tile's no-owner recovery (#94724) awaits a slow cross-backend stored
read after the owner gate fails closed. If a later resumeTile for the same
stored id resumes live in the meantime (the owner backfill stamped the
row), the stale recovery still resolved last and:

- set the global read-only latch over the live outcome, because the live
  resume's clear ran while the latch was not set yet; and
- repainted the tile read-only and repointed the stored -> runtime binding
  onto the synthetic read-only:<id>, so the warm path kept reusing it and
  submits were refused for good.

The main-pane recovery already guards this with isCurrentResume(); the
helper-backed tile path had no analog.

- read-only-transcript: every successful live resume bumps a per-stored-id
  generation (even a no-op clear); the recovery captures it before its
  stored read and only latches if it did not move.
- use-session-tile-delegate: resumeTile bumps a per-stored-id epoch on
  entry; a read-only outcome from a superseded call returns the current
  binding without painting or rebinding.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
